            'body_en' => "In 1955 a colossal basalt lion turned up by chance on a mound in the Afrin valley, sixty-seven kilometres north-west of Aleppo. Excavations followed in 1956, 1962 and 1964 under Maurice Dunand and Feisal Seirafi, and from 1976 under the Syrian archaeologist Ali Abu Assaf, who uncovered the whole temple and dated it, in three building phases, to between about 1300 and 740 BC.\n\nIt stood on the acropolis of a town that had been lived in since the fourth millennium BC and would be lived in until Ottoman times: a settlement of twenty-five hectares, the upper city rising twenty-five metres above the plain, overlooking the routes along the river. The temple belongs to the world of the Syro-Hittite or Neo-Hittite kingdoms — the small states that kept the arts of the Hittite empire alive after it fell around 1190 BC, until the Assyrians absorbed them.\n\nWhich god lived here is not known. Ishtar-Astarte has been proposed, and the storm god Hadad; the lions and sphinxes guarding every face of the building fit either.",
