            'body_en' => "The temple is a long building in three parts, entered on its short side: a porch between two piers, an antechamber, and a square main hall with a raised sanctuary at the back. The whole stands on a platform faced with reliefs, and the lower walls inside and out were lined with basalt slabs — lions, sphinxes, guilloche bands, and false windows with lattice patterns. Sockets in the floor show where wooden screens and panelling once stood; a basin of soft stone outside served the rituals of the court.\n\nThat sequence of porch, hall and holy of holies, on a platform, with carved cherubim and palm-and-lattice ornament, is close enough to the description of Solomon\'s temple in the Book of Kings that one scholar counted thirty-three features of Ain Dara matching sixty-five details of the biblical text. No stone of Solomon\'s building survives; Ain Dara, older and in the same tradition, is the nearest thing to it that could be walked through — which is why its loss was felt so widely.",
